! THE READER FEEDS NOW, 2026-10-18, AND THIS TEST DOES NOT NOTICE.
! Until then every RD restarted the card reader at device index 0, so
! the machine read the same card forever; StretchIO.bsv's "CARD FEED"
! says why that is wrong and what replaced it.  This program still
! passes unchanged, and the reason is worth knowing rather than being
! glad about: its SECOND read of the reader is the SKIP-flagged one,
! which by design leaves core storage untouched, so where the reader
! was positioned never reached a check.  THE OLD BEHAVIOUR WAS NEVER
! TESTED HERE.  programs/card_reader.strap is the program that does
! test it, by reading two different cards and checking both.
!
! io_reader_printer.strap -- exercises the simulated Input-Output
! subsystem (StretchIO.bsv) end to end against StretchExecute.bsv's
! new IoOp case: RD (read from the simulated card reader), W (write to
! the simulated printer), CCW (Copy Control Word, used throughout as
! the only way this v1 model exposes I-O status -- see StretchIO.bsv's
! "NOT PROPAGATED" note), and REL (Release). Also several discriminating
! checks: issuing RD to the wrong device type (Exchange Program Check),
! addressing a channel with no device installed (Unit Not Ready
! Reject), a SKIP-flagged READ (must advance the control word but leave
! core storage untouched), and RELEASE SEOP (must NOT set the EOP
! status bit, unlike plain REL -- this last one is a genuine
! adversarial-distinguishing test: the first implementation of REL
! ignored its own SEOP flag entirely and set EOP unconditionally, a bug
! caught by post-milestone review rather than by a test program; CCWF
! below is what would have shown eop=1 instead of 0 if that bug were
! still present).
!
! Not a Reference Manual worked example -- this project's first
! purpose-written program for a feature the manual doesn't happen to
! walk through a worked instance of. Verified against the manual's own
! stated control-word-update rules (p.124) instead, with the documented
! WRITE "+2" deviation (StretchIO.bsv's file header: this model has no
! double-buffering, so a completed WRITE here leaves the control word's
! data address at A_i+N, not the real hardware's A_i+N+2).
!
! CHANNEL ADDRESSES are written as pre-scaled literals, not the plain
! channel numbers 32/33 -- the Reference Manual (p.111) places the
! 7-bit channel number at instruction bits 12-18 of the 24-bit address
! field, not its low-order bits, so the field's raw value is channel
! number << 5 (derived independently by direct bit-position arithmetic
! and cross-checked by advisor review before this file was written):
!   chanReader  (32) << 5 = 1024
!   chanPrinter (33) << 5 = 1056
! The channel is reached through an index register (X1/X2), not a bare
! literal in the instruction's own address field, specifically so this
! also exercises index arithmetic on the channel-address field, per
! Reference Manual p.111 ("index arithmetic extends over the full 24
! bits of the channel address field") -- not just the fixed literal
! case.
!
! CONTROL WORDS: CTLW1/CTLW2 below are zero-initialized placeholders.
! This assembler's DD statement cannot resolve a label to get DEST's
! or SRC's word address into the control word's dataAddr field (DD's
! literal parser only accepts decimal/(radix)digits, not symbols -- see
! strap_asm.py's parse_dd_literal), so strap/gen_io_tb.py patches the
! real packed control-word values (and the CCW-checks' expected values)
! into the assembled image after assembly, using the same
! packControlWord bit layout as StretchTypes.bsv -- the same reason
! Figure 7's expected results are derived by a companion Python script
! rather than written as STRAP source. See that file for the derivation.

START   LVI, $1, 32      !X1 = reader channel 32: LVI puts it in value bits 0-18,
                         !i.e. value 32<<5 = 1024, the channel field's own position
        LVI, $2, 33      !X2 = printer channel 33 (value 33<<5 = 1056, same reasoning)

        RD, 0($1), CTLW1   !read 3 words from the reader into DEST
        CCW, 0($1), CCWA   !copy the reader's resulting control word

        W, 0($2), CTLW2    !write 2 words from SRC to the printer
        CCW, 0($2), CCWB   !copy the printer's resulting control word

        RD, 0($2), CTLW1   !wrong-unit test: RD given to the printer
        CCW, 0($2), CCWC   !copy the printer's control word (expect EPGK+EOP)

        REL, 0($1), 0      !release the reader channel
        CCW, 0($1), CCWD   !copy the reader's control word (expect EOP only,
                            !dataAddr/count unchanged from CCWA)

        RD, 0($1), CTLW3   !SKIP test: skip-flagged RD -- must advance the
                            !control word's dataAddr/count as usual but must
                            !NOT write DEST2 (still the reader's channel, $1)
        CCW, 0($1), CCWE   !copy the resulting control word

        W(SEOP), 0($2), CTLW4  !WSEOP: 1-word write, EOP suppressed, so the
                                !printer's control word has eop=0 going into
                                !the RELEASE SEOP test below
        REL(SEOP), 0($2), 0    !RELEASE SEOP: must leave eop=0 (unlike plain
                                !REL, which unconditionally sets it)
        CCW, 0($2), CCWF       !copy the printer's control word

        RD, 992, CTLW1     !unit-not-ready test: an UNINSTALLED channel.
                            !992 = 31 << 5, i.e. channel 31 sitting in
                            !bits 12-18 of the 24-bit channel-address
                            !field (Reference Manual p.111) -- the same
                            !derivation the LVI,$1,32 above uses, written
                            !out as a literal because no index is needed.
                            !
                            !THIS USED TO SAY "channel 0, no device
                            !there" AND CHANNEL 0 NOW HAS ONE: it is the
                            !disk (StretchIO.bsv chanDisk, read off MCP
                            !printed pp.15-16's own assembled code).  Any
                            !unattached channel serves this test; the
                            !particular number never mattered, and 31 is
                            !the top of the basic exchange's range.
        BEW, $
        NOP, 0             !pad to a word boundary: DD applies no automatic
                            !alignment (unlike instructions), and the run of
                            !instructions above this point is an odd number
                            !of half-words -- see gen_io_tb.py's word_addr()

CTLW1   DD(BU,64), 0        !patched: dataAddr=DEST, count=3
CTLW2   DD(BU,64), 0        !patched: dataAddr=SRC, count=2
CTLW3   DD(BU,64), 0        !patched: dataAddr=DEST2, count=3, skip=1
CTLW4   DD(BU,64), 0        !patched: dataAddr=SRC, count=1, seop=1
CCWA    DD(BU,64), 0
CCWB    DD(BU,64), 0
CCWC    DD(BU,64), 0
CCWD    DD(BU,64), 0
CCWE    DD(BU,64), 0
CCWF    DD(BU,64), 0
DEST    DD(BU,64), 0,0,0
SRC     DD(BU,64), 111,222
DEST2   DD(BU,64), 3735879681,3735879682,3735879683
                            !sentinel 0xDEAD0001/0002/0003 -- must survive
                            !the SKIP-flagged RD above completely unchanged

        END, START
